      "text": "(a) Given the arithmetic progression, we can use the relationship among successive terms:\n\n$$\na - 10 = b - a = ab - b\n$$\n\nThis algebraic manipulation yields three possible equations:\n\n$$\n(1) \\quad ab - ab = 10 - b\n$$\n\n$$\n(2) \\quad a - 10 - b = ab - ab\n$$\n\n$$\n(3) \\quad 10 - b - a + ab = ab - ab\n$$\n\n(b) Let's analyze each equation one by one.\n\nStarting from equation (1):\n\nThe constant term of $-b$ does not affect the solution, so we can disregard it.\n\nNow, consider equation (2):\n\n$$\na - 10 = ab\n$$\n$$\n(ab - a) - 10 = 0\n$$\n\nFactoring out $a$, we get:\n\n$$\na(b - 1) = 10\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a = \\frac{10}{b - 1}$ into equation (3):\n\n$$\n10 = ab - a - b + ab\n$$\n$$\na(b - 1) = 10\n$$\n\nNow that we have two forms of $a$, we use properties of equations with quadratic expressions:\n\nLet's set $d = b - 1$, which gives us:\n\n$$\na = \\frac{10}{d}\n$$\n\n$a$ and $d$ must be reciprocal to balance our equation, so:\n\n$$\nab = \\frac{10b}{d} = \\frac{10(b - 1)}{d}\n$$\n\nLet’s substitute $a$ back into the original equation (1):\n\n$$\nab - ab = 10 - b\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{d} \\cdot \\frac{10}{d} - \\frac{10}{d} = 10 - b\n$$\n\nSimplify and solve for $b$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{100}{d^2} - \\frac{10}{d} = 10 - b\n$$\n$$\n- \\frac{10d}{d^2} = - b\n$$\n$$\nb = \\frac{10d}{d^2 - 10}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b$ into the equation (3):\n\n$$\n10 = \\frac{10d}{d^2 - 10} - d + \\frac{10d}{d^2 - 10}\n$$\n$$\n10 = d\n$$\n\nNow that we've found $d = 10$, we can solve for $a$:\n\nSince $a = \\frac{10}{d}$:\n\n$$\na = \\frac{10}{10} = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, to count the number of pairs $(a, b)$ that satisfy this solution:\n\n$$\na = 1, b = 10\n$$\n\nThus, there is only one pair of real numbers $(a, b)$ that fits the criteria, equal to $\\boxed{1}$.",
