      "text": "The given arithmetic progression is \\(10, a, b, ab\\). Let's denote the common difference by \\(d\\). Then, we can express the terms in terms of \\(d\\):\n\\[\na = 10 + d, \\quad b = 10 + 2d, \\quad ab = (10 + d)(10 + 2d)\n\\]\nFrom \\(ab = (10 + d)(10 + 2d)\\), we expand it to:\n\\[\nab = 100 + 20d + 10d + 2d^2 = 100 + 30d + 2d^2\n\\]\nSince \\(b = 10 + 2d\\), we substitute \\(b\\) into the equation:\n\\[\n100 + 30d + 2d^2 = (10 + 2d)(10 + 2d)\n\\]\nExpanding the right-hand side, we get:\n\\[\n100 + 30d + 2d^2 = 100 + 40d + 4d^2\n\\]\nRearranging all terms to one side gives us:\n\\[\n100 + 30d + 2d^2 = 100 + 40d + 4d^2 \\implies 2d^2 + 40d + 40 - 2d^2 - 30d = 0 \\implies 10d + 40 = 0\n\\]\nSolving for \\(d\\), we get:\n\\[\nd = -\\frac{40}{10} = -4\n\\]\nNow, substituting \\(d = -4\\) back into the expressions for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\\[\na = 10 + d = 10 - 4 = 6\n\\]\n\\[\nb = 10 + 2d = 10 + 2(-4) = 10 - 8 = 2\n\\]\nThus, the only pair \\((a, b)\\) is \\((6, 2)\\). Therefore, there is only one pair of real numbers \\((a, b)\\) that satisfies the original arithmetic progression condition. The final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
